<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abgaal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abgaal are a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawiye">Hawiye</a> clan family, belonging to its Hiraab branch and living mainly in and around Mogadishu and the middle Shabelle region. In the late 17th century they took part in the revolt that ended the Ajuran Sultanate's control of the coast and formed the Hiraab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abgaal.jpg" alt="Abgaal" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AMISOM, Welimo28, AU-UN IST / STUART PRICE. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mogadishu.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Abgaal belong to the Mudulood, one of the divisions of the Hiraab, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habar-gidir">Habar Gidir</a>, Sheekhaal and Duduble. The clan is regarded as relatively young, its founder dating from the 1500s. A tradition recorded by the Italian scholar Enrico Cerulli makes the founder, Ali Isman, the son of Osman Darandolle of the Hiraab and of Faduma Sarjeele, a woman of the Gareen house that headed the Ajuran Sultanate.</p><p>According to the story, Faduma's father was warned that the boy's descendants would drive out his own and tried to poison him, but the mother drank the poison by mistake. The boy hid behind a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> and called the camels his grandfather, whence the name Abgaal, camel-grandfather. Cerulli read the story as a memory of fighting between Abgaal pastoral nomads coming from the north and the Ajuran.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Clan traditions say that the Abgaal Darandoolle began to arrive in the hinterland of Mogadishu in the sixteenth century, while the Muzaffar dynasty ruled the town, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from Mogadishu record Abgaal nomads in the interior at about the same time. Towards the end of the seventeenth century an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> of Abgaal descent settled in the Shangaani quarter. His lineage, the Yaaquub, intermarried with merchant families of Yemeni origin and became known in the trade between the coast and the interior, and by the 1700s it ruled most of the town.</p><p>Traditions of the fall of the Ajuran speak of heavy taxes and of a quarrel over watering rights, after which the Darandoolle killed the Ajuran imam at a well called Ceel Cawl. According to oral tradition as reported by Lee Cassanelli, the Hiraab Imamate that followed was an alliance of related groups that ruled from the Shabelle valley to the arid lands of Mudug for at least two centuries. Mogadishu was its capital, and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a> its commercial centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Abgaal are divided into three main branches: the Harti, the eldest, the Wacbudhan and the Waceysle, the youngest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> of the Mudulood and of the Hiraab traditionally comes from the Abgaal, from the Owbakar sub-clan, while the ugaas of the Mudulood comes from the Reer Ugaas sub-clan. Mogadishu is regarded as Abgaal territory, and they form the majority of its population. Three presidents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, Ali Mahdi Muhammad, Sharif Sheikh Ahmed and Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, have been Abgaal, as was Daud Abdulle Hirsi, the first commander-in-chief of the Somali National Army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abgaal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Mogadishu region and its hinterland, the Hawiye <b>Abgaal</b> and related clans established the Hiraab Imamate, which, according to tradition, defeated the Ajuran and took over the coast and the central plains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 17th century the Hiraab clans of the Hawiye, among them the <b>Abgaal</b> and Habar Gidir, revolted against the Ajuran Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/hiraab-imamate">Hiraab Imamate</a></li></ul></section>
